# Skip List Implementation in C++

A **skip list** is a probabilistic data structure that provides an alternative to balanced trees, offering **O(log n)** average time for search, insert, and delete operations. It uses multiple layers of linked lists where higher layers act as "express lanes" that skip over elements.

## Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <climits>
#include <vector>

class SkipList {
private:
    struct Node {
        int val;
        std::vector<Node*> forward;  // forward[i] = next node at level i
        Node(int v, int level) : val(v), forward(level + 1, nullptr) {}
    };

    static constexpr int MAX_LEVEL = 16;  // supports up to 2^16 elements efficiently
    static constexpr float P = 0.5f;      // promotion probability

    Node* head;
    int level;  // current highest level in use

    int randomLevel() {
        int lvl = 0;
        while ((float)rand() / RAND_MAX < P && lvl < MAX_LEVEL)
            ++lvl;
        return lvl;
    }

public:
    SkipList() : level(0) {
        head = new Node(-1, MAX_LEVEL);
        srand(static_cast<unsigned>(time(nullptr)));
    }

    ~SkipList() {
        Node* cur = head;
        while (cur) {
            Node* next = cur->forward[0];
            delete cur;
            cur = next;
        }
    }

    // ---------- SEARCH ----------
    bool search(int target) {
        Node* cur = head;
        for (int i = level; i >= 0; --i) {
            while (cur->forward[i] && cur->forward[i]->val < target)
                cur = cur->forward[i];
        }
        cur = cur->forward[0];
        return cur && cur->val == target;
    }

    // ---------- INSERT ----------
    void insert(int num) {
        std::vector<Node*> update(MAX_LEVEL + 1, nullptr);
        Node* cur = head;

        for (int i = level; i >= 0; --i) {
            while (cur->forward[i] && cur->forward[i]->val < num)
                cur = cur->forward[i];
            update[i] = cur;
        }

        cur = cur->forward[0];

        // If value already exists, do nothing (or replace, depending on policy)
        if (cur && cur->val == num) return;

        int newLevel = randomLevel();
        if (newLevel > level) {
            for (int i = level + 1; i <= newLevel; ++i)
                update[i] = head;
            level = newLevel;
        }

        Node* newNode = new Node(num, newLevel);
        for (int i = 0; i <= newLevel; ++i) {
            newNode->forward[i] = update[i]->forward[i];
            update[i]->forward[i] = newNode;
        }
    }

    // ---------- DELETE ----------
    bool erase(int num) {
        std::vector<Node*> update(MAX_LEVEL + 1, nullptr);
        Node* cur = head;

        for (int i = level; i >= 0; --i) {
            while (cur->forward[i] && cur->forward[i]->val < num)
                cur = cur->forward[i];
            update[i] = cur;
        }

        cur = cur->forward[0];
        if (!cur || cur->val != num) return false;  // not found

        // Unlink from every level
        for (int i = 0; i <= level; ++i) {
            if (update[i]->forward[i] != cur) break;
            update[i]->forward[i] = cur->forward[i];
        }
        delete cur;

        // Shrink head level if top levels became empty
        while (level > 0 && !head->forward[level])
            --level;

        return true;
    }

    // Debug helper
    void print() {
        for (int i = level; i >= 0; --i) {
            Node* cur = head->forward[i];
            std::cout << "Level " << i << ": ";
            while (cur) {
                std::cout << cur->val << " ";
                cur = cur->forward[i];
            }
            std::cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    SkipList sl;
    for (int v : {3, 6, 7, 9, 12, 19, 17, 26, 21, 25}) sl.insert(v);
    sl.print();

    std::cout << "Search 19: " << sl.search(19) << "\n";
    std::cout << "Search 15: " << sl.search(15) << "\n";

    sl.erase(19);
    std::cout << "After erase 19:\n";
    sl.print();
    return 0;
}
```

## How It Works

1. **Multiple levels:** Each node has a random height (levels 0..MAX_LEVEL). Level 0 contains all elements; each higher level is a sparser sublist.
2. **Search:** Start at the top-left; at each level move right while the next value is smaller. When you can't move right, drop down a level. Arrive at the insertion position in O(log n) expected steps.
3. **Insert:** Record the "update" (predecessor at each level) during the descent, create the new node with a randomly chosen height, and splice it in at every level up to its height.
4. **Delete:** Same descent to find the node and its predecessors; then unlink it from every level where it appears. Shrink `level` if top levels become empty.

## Complexity Analysis

### Time Complexity (expected)

| Operation | Expected | Worst Case |
|-----------|----------|------------|
| Search    | O(log n) | O(n)       |
| Insert    | O(log n) | O(n)       |
| Delete    | O(log n) | O(n)       |

**Why O(log n) on average?** With promotion probability `p = 0.5`, the expected number of nodes in level `i` is `n · p^i`. A level contains ~1 node when `p^i · n ≈ 1`, i.e., `i ≈ log₁/ₚ n = log₂ n`. The search path visits `O(1/p)` nodes per level across `O(log n)` levels, giving **O(log n)** expected time.

**Worst case O(n):** If `randomLevel()` produces a degenerate distribution (e.g., all single-level), the structure collapses to a linked list.

### Space Complexity

- **Expected: O(n)** — each node's *expected* height is `1/(1-p) = 2` levels, so total pointer storage ≈ `2n`.
- **Worst case: O(n · log n)** — if all nodes reach the maximum height `log n`.

The `MAX_LEVEL = 16` cap with `p = 0.5` comfortably handles up to ~2¹⁶ = 65,536 elements before any meaningful degradation; increase it for very large datasets.

### Practical Notes

- **Deterministic vs randomized:** Skip lists give the same asymptotics as balanced BSTs (AVL/Red-Black) but with far simpler insertion/deletion — no rotations needed.
- **Cache behavior:** Skip lists are pointer-heavy; B-trees are often faster in practice for disk-backed workloads.
- **Concurrency:** Skip lists are naturally amenable to lock-free implementations, which is why they're used in `std::map`-like structures such as Java's `ConcurrentSkipListMap` and Redis' sorted sets.

# Minimal LRU Cache in Go

```go
package lru

import "container/list"

type entry struct {
	key   string
	value any
}

type Cache struct {
	cap   int
	items map[string]*list.Element
	order *list.List // front = most recently used
}

func New(capacity int) *Cache {
	return &Cache{
		cap:   capacity,
		items: make(map[string]*list.Element, capacity),
		order: list.New(),
	}
}

func (c *Cache) Get(key string) (any, bool) {
	if el, ok := c.items[key]; ok {
		c.order.MoveToFront(el)
		return el.Value.(*entry).value, true
	}
	return nil, false
}

func (c *Cache) Put(key string, value any) {
	if el, ok := c.items[key]; ok {
		el.Value.(*entry).value = value
		c.order.MoveToFront(el)
		return
	}
	if c.order.Len() >= c.cap {
		back := c.order.Back()
		c.order.Remove(back)
		delete(c.items, back.Value.(*entry).key)
	}
	el := c.order.PushFront(&entry{key, value})
	c.items[key] = el
}
```

**Why it's O(1):** `map` lookup/insert/delete is amortized O(1); `container/list` operations (`MoveToFront`, `PushFront`, `Remove`, `Back`) are O(1) given the element pointer.

---

## Two ways it fails under load

### 1. No mutex → data race / panic under concurrency

`map` and `list.List` are not goroutine-safe. Concurrent `Get`/`Put` calls will corrupt the map, panic with `concurrent map read and map write`, or corrupt the linked list (dropping or double-removing nodes). Under any real concurrent load this fails immediately. Fix: wrap with a `sync.Mutex` or `sync.RWMutex` (taking the write lock on `Get` too, since it mutates the LRU order).

### 2. Thundering-herd cache stampede on hot keys

When a hot key expires or is evicted, every concurrent request sees a miss and hits the backing store simultaneously. At high QPS this can overload the origin database — precisely the situation the cache was supposed to protect against. Fix: singleflight/mutex-per-key on the loader path, or refresh-ahead on the cache side.

Honorable mentions if you want more: capacity 0 (`Put` on a zero-cap cache never stores anything and `Get` always misses — easily triggered if capacity is loaded from misconfigured env), and lock contention on a single global mutex becoming the bottleneck at high core counts.

