```ts
class LRUCache<K, V> {
  private readonly map = new Map<K, V>();

  constructor(private readonly capacity: number) {
    if (capacity <= 0) throw new Error("capacity must be > 0");
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) return undefined;

    const value = this.map.get(key) as V;
    this.map.delete(key);
    this.map.set(key, value); // refresh recency
    return value;
  }

  put(key: K, value: V): void {
    if (this.map.has(key)) this.map.delete(key);

    this.map.set(key, value);

    if (this.map.size > this.capacity) {
      const oldest = this.map.keys().next().value as K;
      this.map.delete(oldest);
    }
  }
}
```

This is average **O(1)** for `get` and `put`, using JavaScript `Map` insertion order as the LRU order.

Two ways it could fail under load:

1. **Memory blow-up despite bounded entry count**  
   Capacity limits number of entries, not bytes. A few large values, or high-cardinality keys with large payloads, can still cause OOM or GC thrashing under load.

2. **Cache stampede / thundering herd**  
   When a hot key is evicted or missing, many concurrent requests can all miss and recompute/fetch the same value. There is no single-flight/coalescing here, so downstream load can spike exactly when the cache is under pressure.

