 * `java.util.Arrays.parallelPrefix`, but written with Kotlin coroutines and
 * generic over the accumulator type (Long here).
 *
 * ## Tricky parts, called out:
 *
 * - **Structured concurrency gives us memory visibility.** Each `launch` inside
 *   `coroutineScope` happens-before the scope returns. That is what makes the
 *   `chunkTotals[chunkIdx] = ...` writes in step 2 visible to the sequential
 *   step 4, without needing `@Volatile` or atomics. Do *not* replace this with
 *   a raw thread pool and a plain array unless you re-add explicit
 *   synchronization.
 *
 * - **Each coroutine writes a disjoint slice of `result`.** That is why we can
 *   avoid locks entirely. If you ever change the chunking to overlap (e.g. for
 *   a Blelloch-style up/down sweep), you must add barriers between passes.
 *
 * - **Sequential fallback for small inputs.** Coroutine dispatch costs more
 *   than the scan itself below roughly a few thousand elements. The threshold
 *   below is deliberately conservative; tune for your workload.
 *
 * - **Overflow.** Matches a sequential scan: `Long` arithmetic wraps. If you
 *   need checked arithmetic, use `Math.addExact` (slower) or promote to
 *   `BigInteger` in the outer scan only — but note the *inner* per-chunk scan
 *   still wraps in the same way a sequential scan would.
 *
 * - **The caller's array is not modified.** We copy up front. If you can
 *   safely mutate in place, drop the `copyOf()` and write directly into
 *   `input` — but then the API must say so loudly.
 *
 * - **Chunk count `p` is capped by the work available.** Spawning one coroutine
 *   per element would be slower than sequential. We cap `p` at
 *   `n / MIN_CHUNK_SIZE` (and at the caller's `parallelism`).
 *
 * ## Alternatives worth knowing about
 *
 * - `java.util.Arrays.parallelPrefix(long[], LongBinaryOperator)` from the JDK:
 *   zero-dependency, uses a tree-shaped ForkJoin scan. Prefer it if you only
 *   need primitives and don't need Kotlin idioms or coroutine cancellation.
 * - Hillis–Steele: `log2(n)` parallel steps, each a shift-and-add. Simpler to
 *   reason about, but O(n log n) work — usually slower in practice.
 * - Blelloch: work-efficient O(n) but two tree passes and non-intuitive
 *   indexing; worth it only when span matters more than constant factors.
 *
 * @param input       source array; not mutated
 * @param parallelism max number of concurrent chunks; defaults to #cores
 * @return a new array `b` with `b[i] = input[0] + ... + input[i]`
 */
suspend fun parallelPrefixSum(
    input: LongArray,
    parallelism: Int = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(),
): LongArray {
    require(parallelism >= 1) { "parallelism must be >= 1, was $parallelism" }
    val n = input.size
    if (n == 0) return LongArray(0)
    if (n == 1) return longArrayOf(input[0])

    // Fall back to sequential below this size: dispatch overhead dominates.
    if (n < PARALLEL_THRESHOLD || parallelism == 1) {
        return sequentialPrefixSum(input)
    }

    // Cap the number of chunks so each chunk has real work to do.
    val maxUsefulChunks = maxOf(1, n / MIN_CHUNK_SIZE)
    val chunkCount = minOf(parallelism, maxUsefulChunks)
    if (chunkCount == 1) {
        return sequentialPrefixSum(input)
    }

    val chunkSize = (n + chunkCount - 1) / chunkCount  // ceil
    val result = input.copyOf()
    val chunkTotals = LongArray(chunkCount)

    // Pass 1 — local inclusive scan per chunk, in parallel.
    coroutineScope {
        for (chunkIdx in 0 until chunkCount) {
            launch(Dispatchers.Default) {
                val start = chunkIdx * chunkSize
                val end = minOf(start + chunkSize, n)
                var acc = 0L
                for (i in start until end) {
                    acc += input[i]
                    result[i] = acc
                }
                chunkTotals[chunkIdx] = acc
            }
        }
    }

    // Pass 2 — exclusive scan of chunk totals. `chunkCount` is O(cores),
    // so a plain loop is faster than anything fancier here.
    val chunkOffsets = LongArray(chunkCount)
    var running = 0L
    for (i in 0 until chunkCount) {
        chunkOffsets[i] = running
        running += chunkTotals[i]
    }

    // Pass 3 — add offsets back into each chunk, in parallel.
    coroutineScope {
        for (chunkIdx in 1 until chunkCount) { // chunk 0 offset is always 0
            launch(Dispatchers.Default) {
                val offset = chunkOffsets[chunkIdx]
                val start = chunkIdx * chunkSize
                val end = minOf(start + chunkSize, n)
                for (i in start until end) {
                    result[i] += offset
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return result
}

