# Reservoir Sampler in Rust

Here's a production-quality implementation of reservoir sampling (Algorithm R), with careful attention to the tricky parts.

```rust
use rand::Rng;
use std::fmt;

/// A reservoir sampler that keeps a uniform random sample of up to `k`
/// items from a stream of unknown length.
///
/// Uses Algorithm R (Vitter, 1985). After observing `n` items, every
/// size-`k` subset of the observed items is equally likely to be the
/// reservoir. Memory is `O(k)`; time is `O(1)` amortized per item.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use rand::rngs::StdRng;
/// use rand::SeedableRng;
///
/// let mut rng = StdRng::seed_from_u64(42);
/// let mut sampler = ReservoirSampler::new(3, rng);
///
/// for i in 0..1000 {
///     sampler.observe(i);
/// }
///
/// assert_eq!(sampler.len(), 3);
/// let sample: Vec<i32> = sampler.into_iter().copied().collect();
/// # let _ = sample;
/// ```
pub struct ReservoirSampler<T, R> {
    reservoir: Vec<T>,
    capacity: usize,
    /// Number of items observed. Saturating at `usize::MAX`, which on
    /// 64-bit systems means "more than we'll ever stream".
    seen: usize,
    rng: R,
}

impl<T, R: Rng> ReservoirSampler<T, R> {
    /// Creates a new sampler with reservoir capacity `k`.
    ///
    /// `k == 0` is legal and yields an empty reservoir; every `observe`
    /// call is a no-op after the RNG future-proofing below.
    pub fn new(k: usize, rng: R) -> Self {
        Self {
            reservoir: Vec::with_capacity(k),
            capacity: k,
            seen: 0,
            rng,
        }
    }

    /// Observes one item from the stream.
    ///
    /// The first `k` items are admitted unconditionally. Afterwards, the
    /// `n`-th item (0-indexed) is admitted with probability `k/(n+1)`.
    /// When admitted, it replaces a uniformly chosen slot, keeping the
    /// sample uniform.
    ///
    /// # Complexity
    ///
    /// One `usize` increment and one RNG draw per call, plus a `Vec`
    /// write with probability `k/n`.
    pub fn observe(&mut self, item: T) {
        // Always advance the counter, even after the reservoir is full.
        // This invariant simplifies reasoning: `seen` == number observed.
        let n = self.seen;
        self.seen = n.saturating_add(1);

        if n < self.capacity {
            self.reservoir.push(item);
            return;
        }

        // `n + 1` is the count *including* this item (with `n = seen`
        // before increment). We admit with probability `k / (n + 1)`.
        //
        // TRICKY: `n + 1` can overflow `usize` after ~2^64 items on a
        // 64-bit machine. We use the post-saturating `seen` value and
        // reason as if the stream were infinite past that point —
        // acceptable in practice (a stream of 2^64 items is not
        // something we'll actually see), but documented because it's a
        // correctness cliff for adversarial use.
        let new_count = self.seen; // == min(n + 1, usize::MAX), effectively
        if new_count == 0 {
            // Overflowed past `usize::MAX`. Degenerate; admit nothing.
            return;
        }

        // Draw a candidate slot from `0..new_count`. If it lands in the
        // first `k` slots, item replaces that slot; otherwise, drop it.
        // This is the standard Algorithm R trick and is equivalent to
        // "admit with prob k/n, then pick a uniform slot".
        //
        // TRICKY: use `gen_range` (inclusive-exclusive) — `%` is biased
        // for non-power-of-two ranges and would silently skew samples.
        let slot = self.rng.gen_range(0..new_count);
        if slot < self.capacity {
            self.reservoir[slot] = item;
        }
    }

    /// Returns the current reservoir contents in insertion order.
    pub fn as_slice(&self) -> &[T] {
        &self.reservoir
    }

    /// Number of items currently in the reservoir (≤ `k`).
    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.reservoir.len()
    }

    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.reservoir.is_empty()
    }

    /// Total number of items observed so far (saturating).
    pub fn total_seen(&self) -> usize {
        self.seen
    }

    /// Consumes the sampler, returning the reservoir.
    pub fn into_vec(self) -> Vec<T> {
        self.reservoir
    }
}

impl<T, R> IntoIterator for ReservoirSampler<T, R> {
    type Item = T;
    type IntoIter = std::vec::IntoIter<T>;
    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {
        self.reservoir.into_iter()
    }
}

impl<T: fmt::Debug, R> fmt::Debug for ReservoirSampler<T, R> {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        f.debug_struct("ReservoirSampler")
            .field("reservoir", &self.reservoir)
            .field("capacity", &self.capacity)
            .field("seen", &self.seen)
            .finish_non_exhaustive() // omit the RNG
    }
}
```

## The tricky parts, called out

**1. `k / (n+1)`, not `k / n`.** Off-by-one here is the single most common bug. When considering the `n`-th item (0-indexed), `n+1` items have been observed *including it*. The replacement probability is `k / (n+1)`; picking a uniform integer in `0..n+1` and replacing slot `i` if `i < k` is exactly equivalent and avoids two RNG draws.

**2. Rejection via `slot < k` rather than `rng.gen::<f64>() < k as f64 / n`.** The float approach loses precision for very large `n` (probability underflows, sample silently becomes stale-but-not-uniform). Integer sampling via `gen_range(0..n+1)` is exact.

**3. `gen_range` not `% n`.** Modulo bias is small but real. `rand::Rng::gen_range` uses Lemire's method and is unbiased. In production, you don't want a subtle skew in a "uniform" sample.

**4. `seen` saturates; the reservoir never panics.** A stream theoretically longer than `usize::MAX` items will keep producing a uniform sample of the prefix observed up to saturation, but the *rate* of replacement silently drops to zero. For any realistic service this is fine; it's documented because a reviewer will ask. If you truly need unbounded counts, swap `seen: usize` for `u64` or `u128` and pay one extra branch.

**5. `k == 0` is defined, not a panic.** Callers occasionally configure zero samples for "discover later" workflows; panicking would be brittle. The empty-reservoir path is O(1).

**6. The RNG is a generic parameter, not a hidden `ThreadRng`.** Locking the RNG type at construction means the sampler is `Send + Sync` iff the RNG is, and it's deterministic under `SeedableRng` — critical for reproducible tests and replay debugging. Do not reach for `rand::thread_rng()` inside `observe`: a long stream would contend on the thread-local RNG and pin the sampler to a single thread.

**7. No `Clone` bound on `T`.** Items are moved into the reservoir; when they lose the slot race they are dropped immediately. This matters when streaming large objects (byte buffers, decoded records) — reservoir sampling should not require cloning.

**8. `into_iter` yields insertion order, not sample order.** The convergence of Algorithm R doesn't depend on order, but if downstream code assumes the sample is ordered by stream position, document it — it isn't.

## Note on larger `k`

For `k` in the millions, consider **Algorithm L** (Li, 1994), which skips ahead in geometric jumps — `O(k(1 + log(n/k)))` RNG draws instead of `O(n)`. Algorithm R's one RNG draw per item is fine for typical `k` (hundreds to low thousands) and vastly simpler to get right. If benchmarks show RNG draw dominating, switch to L; don't start there.

# Dijkstra's Shortest Path — Adjacency List + Binary Heap

Here's a production-quality implementation. The tricky parts (overflow, lazily-deleted heap entries, `int64_t` distances) are documented inline.

```cpp
// dijkstra.hpp
#pragma once

#include <cstdint>
#include <limits>
#include <queue>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

namespace graph {

constexpr std::int64_t kInf = std::numeric_limits<std::int64_t>::max();

// Weighted directed edge. Non-negative weights are a precondition.
struct Edge {
    int to;
    std::int64_t weight;
};

using AdjacencyList = std::vector<std::vector<Edge>>;

// Returns shortest distances from `source` to every node.
// Unreachable nodes get kInf.
//
// Preconditions:
//   * 0 <= source < adj.size()
//   * every edge weight >= 0  (Dijkstra's invariant; UB / wrong results otherwise)
//   * no path weight exceeds int64_t::max() (see "overflow" note below)
//
// Complexity: O((V + E) log V) time, O(V) auxiliary space.
inline std::vector<std::int64_t> dijkstra(const AdjacencyList& adj, int source) {
    const int n = static_cast<int>(adj.size());
    std::vector<std::int64_t> dist(n, kInf);

    // Min-heap of (distance, node). We push multiple entries per node and
    // discard stale ones on pop -- this is the standard "lazy deletion"
    // technique. It's simpler and often faster than decrease-key with an
    // indexed heap, and it avoids keeping a position map in sync.
    using Item = std::pair<std::int64_t, int>;          // (dist, node)
    std::priority_queue<Item, std::vector<Item>, std::greater<>> pq;

    dist[source] = 0;
    pq.emplace(0, source);

    while (!pq.empty()) {
        auto [d, u] = pq.top();
        pq.pop();

        // Stale entry: we already found a shorter path to `u` after this
        // entry was pushed. Skip it. Without this check the algorithm is
        // still correct but does O(E) redundant work.
        if (d != dist[u]) continue;

        for (const Edge& e : adj[u]) {
            // Overflow guard: if dist[u] is finite but adding e.weight
            // would wrap int64_t, treat the edge as non-improving. Real
            // deployments usually cap weights well below this.
            if (dist[u] > kInf - e.weight) continue;

            const std::int64_t nd = dist[u] + e.weight;
            if (nd < dist[e.to]) {
                dist[e.to] = nd;
                pq.emplace(nd, e.to);
            }
        }
    }

    return dist;
}

}  // namespace graph
```

## Usage example

```cpp
#include "dijkstra.hpp"

int main() {
    graph::AdjacencyList g(5);
    //               from  to  weight
    g[0].push_back({1, 4});
    g[0].push_back({2, 1});
    g[2].push_back({1, 2});
    g[1].push_back({3, 1});
    g[2].push_back({3, 5});
    g[3].push_back({4, 3});

    auto d = graph::dijkstra(g, /*source=*/0);
    // d == {0, 3, 1, 4, 7}
}
```

## Design notes

**Why `int64_t` distances and `kInf = max()`?**
Using `INT64_MAX` as sentinel means `dist[u] + w` can overflow when `u` is unreachable. We guard it with `if (dist[u] > kInf - e.weight) continue;`. If your domain caps path lengths (e.g., sums of ≤10⁹ edges each ≤10⁹), you can drop the guard. If you prefer, an alternative is to use `kInf = INT64_MAX/4` and skip the check — pick one and be consistent.

**Lazy deletion vs. decrease-key.**
`std::priority_queue` has no decrease-key. The idiom is to push an updated `(dist, node)` and let stale entries be discarded via the `if (d != dist[u])` guard. This is optimal: total pushes are bounded by edges relaxed, and each pop is O(log V). An indexed binary heap with decrease-key is asymptotically equivalent but more code and, in benchmarking dominated by cache behavior, rarely wins.

**`std::greater<>` on `pair<int64_t,int>`.**
Pairs compare lexicographically, so ties break by node id. This is fine (and deterministic). If you care about micro-perf, a custom `struct` with a comparator avoids pulling in tuple-style comparison, but it's not important in practice.

**Adjacency list layout.** `vector<vector<Edge>>` is the idiomatic choice. For very large graphs a flat CSR (`offsets` + `neighbors` + `weights`) gives much better cache behaviour — worth considering if your `|E|` is in the millions and this is hot-path code. The algorithm is otherwise identical.

**Directed vs. undirected.** This builds a directed graph. For undirected, add both `u→v` and `v→u` edges when populating the list.

**Production hardening checklist**
- Assert on negative weights in debug builds (`assert(e.weight >= 0)`).
- If `source` can be invalid, either return all-`kInf` or throw — pick a policy and document it.
- If input graphs are large and untrusted, avoid pathological priority_queue growth by capping edge counts at parse time; the algorithm itself is memory-bounded by `O(E)` pushes.
- For repeated queries on an unchanged graph, consider precomputing with Johnson's/Betweenness, or use A*/bidirectional Dijkstra if you always have a target.

# Thread-safe bounded blocking queue in Kotlin

**TL;DR:** for production, use `java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue`. It is battle-tested, JIT-friendly, and already satisfies the exact contract you described. Only roll your own if you need custom behavior (metrics, priority, extensions, etc.). Below is a correct hand-rolled version, then a walkthrough of the tricky parts — those are the same tricks `ArrayBlockingQueue` uses internally, so it doubles as a template.

## Production baseline (just use this)

```kotlin
val queue: BlockingQueue<Job> = ArrayBlockingQueue(1024) // fair=false by default
queue.put(job)          // blocks while full, interruptible
val job = queue.take()  // blocks while empty, interruptible
```

That is what you should ship. Everything below is for when you *can't*.

---

## Hand-rolled implementation

```kotlin
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock

/**
 * Bounded FIFO, thread-safe, with blocking [put]/[take].
 * Mirrors `java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue` semantics (thread-based, interruptible).
 *
 * Delay to [java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue] unless you need to extend it.
 */
class BoundedBlockingQueue<T : Any>(
    private val capacity: Int,
    fair: Boolean = false,
) {
    init { require(capacity > 0) { "capacity must be > 0, was $capacity" } }

    private val lock = ReentrantLock(fair)

    // Two conditions, not one. See "Tricky parts" — this is the load-bearing trick.
    private val notFull  = lock.newCondition()
    private val notEmpty = lock.newCondition()

    // Generic array via erasure escape hatch. Slots nulled on take to avoid retaining items.
    private val buffer = arrayOfNulls<Any?>(capacity)
    private var head = 0
    private var tail = 0
    private var count = 0

    /** Blocks while full. Interruptible. */
    fun put(item: T) {
        lock.lockInterruptibly()
        try {
            while (count == capacity) notFull.await() // releases lock, re-acquires on wake
            buffer[tail] = item
            tail = (tail + 1) % capacity
            count++
            notEmpty.signal()                        // exactly one consumer can now proceed
        } finally {
            lock.unlock()
        }
    }

    /** Blocks while empty. Interruptible. */
    @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
    fun take(): T {
        lock.lockInterruptibly()
        try {
            while (count == 0) notEmpty.await()
            val item = buffer[head] as T
            buffer[head] = null                       // break reference → GC can reclaim
            head = (head + 1) % capacity
            count--
            notFull.signal()                          // exactly one producer can now proceed
            return item
        } finally {
            lock.unlock()
        }
    }

    // BlokcingQueue-compatible non-blocking / timed variants
    fun offer(item: T): Boolean = lock.withLock {
        if (count == capacity) return false
        buffer[tail] = item
        tail = (tail + 1) % capacity
        count++
        notEmpty.signal()
        true
    }

    fun offer(item: T, timeout: Long, unit: TimeUnit): Boolean {
        var nanos = unit.toNanos(timeout)
        lock.lockInterruptibly()
        try {
            while (count == capacity) {
                if (nanos <= 0L) return false
                nanos = notFull.awaitNanos(nanos)
            }
            buffer[tail] = item
            tail = (tail + 1) % capacity
            count++
            notEmpty.signal()
            return true
        } finally {
            lock.unlock()
        }
    }

    fun poll(): T? = lock.withLock {
        @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
        if (count == 0) return null
        val item = buffer[head] as T
        buffer[head] = null
        head = (head + 1) % capacity
        count--
        notFull.signal()
        item
    }

    fun poll(timeout: Long, unit: TimeUnit): T? {
        var nanos = unit.toNanos(timeout)
        lock.lockInterruptibly()
        try {
            while (count == 0) {
                if (nanos <= 0L) return null
                nanos = notEmpty.awaitNanos(nanos)
            }
            @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
            val item = buffer[head] as T
            buffer[head] = null
            head = (head + 1) % capacity
            count--
            notFull.signal()
            return item
        } finally {
            lock.unlock()
        }
    }

    val size: Int      get() = lock.withLock { count }
    val isEmpty: Boolean get() = lock.withLock { count == 0 }
    val isFull: Boolean  get() = lock.withLock { count == capacity }
}
```

---

## Tricky parts and why they matter

### 1. Two `Condition`s, not one — the whole reason this is fast
If you share a single condition, `put` on a full queue would block, and any subsequent `take` would call `signalAll()` and wake *both* consumers and producers. Most wake up, recheck their predicate, and go back to sleep. That's a "thundering herd" — wasted scheduler churn and CPU under contention. One condition per waiter class (not-full, not-empty) means each signal wakes exactly the party that can make progress.

### 2. `while`, not `if` — spurious wakeups are real
`Condition.await()` may return without its predicate being true (spurious wakeup, interruption-then-cleared, etc.). Also, when the lock is reacquired, another thread may have already consumed the slot. The `while` loop is the only correct forever-form: **always recheck the predicate under the lock**. `if` is a latent bug that appears once a quarter under load.

### 3. `signal()`, not `signalAll()`
Exactly one consumer can drain a slot and exactly one producer can fill a slot per operation, so `signal()` is sufficient and much cheaper. `signalAll()` is a correctness-safe fallback but a throughput killer. Note that `signal()` on a condition with no waiters is a no-op — that's fine.

### 4. Always `await()` **inside** the lock, and always `unlock` in `finally`
`await()` atomically releases the lock and re-acquires it on wake — this is the entire point of `Condition`. But `await()` (and `lockInterruptibly()`) throw `InterruptedException`, so the `unlock()` must be in `finally`, or a single interrupt leaks the lock and the queue deadlocks. `withLock { }` is fine for the non-blocking paths; for `put`/`take` you generally want explicit `lockInterruptibly()` so that *waiting for the lock itself* is interruptible too.

### 5. Mutate state **before** `signal()`
State changes and the signal must happen atomically under the lock. The lock's memory barrier then establishes a happens-before edge: a thread woken by `signal()` will see the buffer write that freed the slot. Moving `signal()` before the state mutation silently corrupts the queue — the woken thread sees the stale state, immediately re-blocks, and the mutation happens invisibly.

### 6. Null out consumed slots
`buffer[head] = null` on `take`. Without it, the array retains references to every consumed item until the slot is overwritten, which for a large-capacity long-lived queue is a memory leak in disguise.

### 7. `fair = true` is a real trade-off, not a freebie
Fair `ReentrantLock` essentially guarantees FIFO ordering among waiters, at the cost of significantly higher lock overhead (no barging, more context switches). Use it only if starvation is an actual correctness concern (e.g. mixed producer/consumer rates where some threads must not monopolize). Default `false` for throughput.

### 8. `lockInterruptibly()` and `await()` throw
Thread-based blocking queues are interruptible by design — `BlockingQueue.put` is declared `throws InterruptedException`. Kotlin does not check exceptions, but you should still be aware: caller must handle `InterruptedException` (usually: restore interrupt flag and propagate). If you *never* want interruption (rare, and a smell in most services), you'd use `lockInterruptibly` still and swallow-and-re-raise; hiding interrupts is almost always a bug.

### 9. Non-null `T` bound
`T : Any` matches `BlockingQueue`'s contract (which forbids `null`). This lets callers do `val x: T = queue.take()` without `!!` or `?:`, and prevents ambiguity with `poll()`'s null-return on empty. If you genuinely need nullable items, wrap in `Optional` and use `ArrayDeque<Optional<T>>` — don't reopen the null-everywhere question.

### 10. False sharing (micro-optimization, usually skip)
`head`, `tail`, `count`, and `buffer` reference live on the same cache line. Under extreme contention on many-core sockets this can degrade throughput. JDK code sometimes pads these; user code usually shouldn't. If profiling shows it, split the fields into `@Contended` holders — but *only* if profiling shows it.

### 11. Coroutines are not threads
If your service is already `kotlinx.coroutines`-based, use `Channel<T>(capacity)` (or `Channel(capacity, onBufferOverflow = BufferOverflow.SUSPEND)`) instead — `send`/`receive` are suspending and cooperate with structured concurrency. Mixing thread-blocking queues into coroutine code is a common source of thread-pool starvation: a coroutine blocked in `put()` holds a thread that could be running other coroutines. Pick one concurrency model at the boundary.

---

### When *not* to roll your own

- **Metrics / observability hooks** — subclasses of `ArrayBlockingQueue` or wrapping it with a `BlockingQueue` delegate is almost always cleaner.
- **Priorities** — `PriorityBlockingQueue` (unbounded though; add an outer semaphore for bounded).
- **Very high throughput, small items** — `Disruptor` (LMAX) or `JCTools` lock-free queues.
- **Producer/consumer stages** — `kotlinx.coroutines` pipeline with `Channel`.

For everything else, `ArrayBlockingQueue(capacity)` is the answer, and the above code is why it's correct.

