# Parsing ISO 8601 Durations in Kotlin

ISO 8601 durations have the format `PnYnMnDTnHnMnS` where components are optional. This implementation handles the common subset (years, months, days, hours, minutes, seconds), treating years as 365 days and months as 30 days for a deterministic result.

## Implementation

```kotlin
import java.time.Duration

private val DURATION_REGEX = Regex(
    """^P(?:(\d+)Y)?(?:(\d+)M)?(?:(\d+)W)?(?:(\d+)D)?(?:T(?:(\d+)H)?(?:(\d+)M)?(?:(\d+)S)?)?$"""
)

/**
 * Parses an ISO 8601 duration string into total seconds.
 *
 * Supports: PnYnMnWnDTnHnMnS (all components optional except at least one).
 * Years = 365 days, months = 30 days (per ISO 8601 estimate conventions).
 *
 * Throws IllegalArgumentException for malformed input.
 */
fun parseIsoDurationToSeconds(input: String): Long {
    require(input.isNotEmpty()) { "Duration string cannot be empty" }
    require(input[0] == 'P') { "ISO 8601 duration must start with 'P': $input" }

    // 'T' must not appear without a time component after it, and P alone is invalid.
    require(input != "P" && input != "PT") { "Duration must contain at least one component: $input" }

    val match = DURATION_REGEX.matchEntire(input)
        ?: throw IllegalArgumentException("Invalid ISO 8601 duration: $input")

    val (years, months, weeks, days, hours, minutes, seconds) =
        match.destructured.toList().map { it.toLongOrNull() ?: 0L }

    val HAS_T = input.contains('T')
    // Regex already guarantees structure; but sanity check PnH format (time without T)
    require(!(HAS_T && (hours == 0L && minutes == 0L && seconds == 0L) && match.groups[5] == null && match.groups[6] == null && match.groups[7] == null)) {
        "'T' must be followed by at least one time component: $input"
    }

    val totalDays = years * 365 + months * 30 + weeks * 7 + days
    return totalDays * 86_400L + hours * 3_600L + minutes * 60L + seconds
}
```

