Below is a PostgreSQL implementation. It parses a single CSV record/line into a `text[]`.

It handles:

- comma-separated fields
- quoted fields containing commas
- doubled quotes inside quoted fields: `""` becomes `"`
- empty fields
- quoted empty fields (`""`)
- explicit errors for malformed CSV such as unterminated quotes or unexpected quotes

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION parse_csv_line(line text)
RETURNS text[]
LANGUAGE plpgsql
AS $$
DECLARE
    n             int := char_length(line);
    i             int := 1;
    field_start   int := 1;

    c             text;
    next_c        text;
    raw           text;
    val           text;

    in_quotes     boolean := false;
    was_quoted    boolean := false;

    result        text[] := ARRAY[]::text[];
BEGIN
    WHILE i <= n LOOP
        c := substr(line, i, 1);

        IF in_quotes THEN
            IF c = '"' THEN
                next_c := CASE
                              WHEN i < n THEN substr(line, i + 1, 1)
                              ELSE NULL
                          END;

                IF next_c = '"' THEN
                    -- Escaped quote inside a quoted field: ""
                    i := i + 2;
                ELSE
                    -- Closing quote
                    in_quotes := false;

                    -- After a closing quote, the next character must be comma or end-of-line
                    IF i < n AND substr(line, i + 1, 1) <> ',' THEN
                        RAISE EXCEPTION
                            'Invalid character after closing quote at position %', i + 1;
                    END IF;

                    i := i + 1; -- consume closing quote
                END IF;
            ELSE
                -- Ordinary character inside quoted field, including commas
                i := i + 1;
            END IF;

        ELSE
            IF c = '"' THEN
                -- A quoted field must begin with a quote at the start of the field
                IF i = field_start THEN
                    was_quoted := true;
                    in_quotes := true;
                ELSE
                    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unexpected quote at position %', i;
                END IF;

                i := i + 1;

            ELSIF c = ',' THEN
                -- End of current field
                raw := substr(line, field_start, i - field_start);

                IF was_quoted THEN
                    -- Remove surrounding quotes, then unescape doubled quotes
                    val := substr(raw, 2, char_length(raw) - 2);
                    val := replace(val, '""', '"');
                ELSE
                    val := raw;
                END IF;

                result := array_append(result, val);

                field_start := i + 1;
                was_quoted := false;
                i := i + 1;

            ELSE
                i := i + 1;
            END IF;
        END IF;
    END LOOP;

    IF in_quotes THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unterminated quoted field in CSV line: %', line;
    END IF;

    -- Append last field
    raw := CASE
               WHEN field_start <= n
               THEN substr(line, field_start, n - field_start + 1)
               ELSE ''
           END;

    IF was_quoted THEN
        val := substr(raw, 2, char_length(raw) - 2);
        val := replace(val, '""', '"');
    ELSE
        val := raw;
    END IF;

    result := array_append(result, val);

    RETURN result;
END;
$$;
```

Example:

```sql
SELECT parse_csv_line('1,"Smith, John","He said ""Hi"""');
```

Result:

```text
{1,"Smith, John","He said ""Hi"""}
```

To view individual fields:

```sql
SELECT *
FROM unnest(parse_csv_line('1,"Smith, John","He said ""Hi"""')) AS field;
```

Result:

```text
1
Smith, John
He said "Hi"
```

Another example with empty fields:

```sql
SELECT *
FROM unnest(parse_csv_line('a,,c,""'));
```

Result:

```text
a

c

```

Here the third value is `c`, and the fourth is an empty string.

### Complexity

Let `n` be the length of the CSV line in characters.

#### Time complexity

The parser scans the input line once:

```text
O(n)
```

Each character is examined once, and escaped quotes `""` are skipped together.  
Unescaping quoted fields uses `replace(val, '""', '"')`, and the total length of all unescaped fields is at most `O(n)`, so the overall time remains:

```text
O(n)
```

#### Space complexity

The returned array stores all parsed fields. If the total unescaped output length is `m`, where `m <= n`, then space is:

```text
O(m) = O(n)
```

The parser also uses a constant number of scalar variables, ignoring the returned result. Therefore:

```text
Time:  O(n)
Space: O(n)
```

This implementation parses one CSV record at a time. If your CSV contains quoted fields with embedded newlines, you need to parse the entire CSV document as a unit or pre-buffer records until quotes are balanced.

