```go
package ratelimit

import (
	"sync"
	"time"
)

type TokenBucket struct {
	mu       sync.Mutex
	tokens   float64
	capacity float64
	rate     float64 // tokens per second
	last     time.Time
}

func NewTokenBucket(capacity int, ratePerSecond float64) *TokenBucket {
	return &TokenBucket{
		tokens:   float64(capacity),
		capacity: float64(capacity),
		rate:     ratePerSecond,
		last:     time.Now(),
	}
}

func (b *TokenBucket) Allow() bool {
	b.mu.Lock()
	defer b.mu.Unlock()

	now := time.Now()
	elapsed := now.Sub(b.last).Seconds()

	if elapsed > 0 {
		b.tokens += elapsed * b.rate
		if b.tokens > b.capacity {
			b.tokens = b.capacity
		}
		b.last = now
	}

	if b.tokens >= 1 {
		b.tokens--
		return true
	}
	return false
}
```

Two ways this could fail under load:

1. **Mutex contention**: every `Allow()` call takes one global mutex. At high concurrency, goroutines serialize on that lock, so the limiter can become a throughput bottleneck and add latency even when traffic is under the intended rate.

2. **Per-process limiter only**: each service instance has its own bucket. With N replicas, the effective aggregate limit becomes roughly `N * rate`, so horizontal scaling or load balancing can allow far more traffic than intended unless you use a shared/distributed limiter.

