async function fetchAll(urls) {
  const out = [];
  urls.forEach(async (u) => {
    const r = await fetch(u);
    out.push(await r.json());
  });
  return out;
}
```

**Bug:** `Array.prototype.forEach` does **not** await the async callback. `fetchAll` immediately returns the still-empty `out` array, while the fetches keep running in the background.

```js
async function fetchAll(urls) {
  const out = [];
  urls.forEach(async (u) => {
    const r = await fetch(u);
    out.push(await r.json());
  });
  return out; // returns before any fetch/json completes
}
```

So callers typically get `[]`, or see the array mutate unpredictably later. The result order is also nondeterministic.

**Fix:** use `map` + `Promise.all`, which awaits all requests and preserves URL order:

```js
async function fetchAll(urls) {
  return Promise.all(
    urls.map(async (u) => {
      const r = await fetch(u);
      return r.json();
    })
  );
}
```

Or more concisely:

```js
async function fetchAll(urls) {
  return Promise.all(urls.map(async (u) => {
    const r = await fetch(u);
    return r.json();
  }));
}
```

