The accumulator starts as `{}`. Then:

```ts
acc[x[key]]
```

is an attempt to index `{}` with `x[key]`, whose type is roughly `T[keyof T]`. TypeScript cannot prove that value is a valid property key, and `{}` has no index signature. You get errors like:

> Element implicitly has an 'any' type because expression of type `T[keyof T]` can't be used to index type `{}`.

Also, `T[keyof T]` is not constrained to `string | number | symbol`, so it may not even be usable as a computed property key.

The return type is also poor: even if it compiled loosely, callers get `{}`-ish typing, not something like `Record<string, T[]>` or `Map<K, T[]>`.

---

### 2. It breaks on inherited object properties at runtime

Even if you cast around the TypeScript errors, this uses a plain object `{}` as the accumulator:

```ts
acc[x[key]] ||= []
```

If the grouping key is any inherited `Object.prototype` property name, `acc[key]` is already truthy but is not an array. Then `.push()` explodes.

Examples:

```ts
groupBy([{ kind: "toString" }], "kind")
// acc["toString"] is Object.prototype.toString, truthy
// (function).push is not a function -> TypeError

groupBy([{ kind: "constructor" }], "kind")
// acc["constructor"] is Object, truthy -> TypeError

groupBy([{ kind: "__proto__" }], "kind")
// acc["__proto__"] is Object.prototype, truthy -> TypeError
```

So it breaks as soon as a group value coerces to `"toString"`, `"constructor"`, `"hasOwnProperty"`, `"valueOf"`, `"__proto__"`, etc.

Using `??=` instead of `||=` does not fix this, because inherited properties are non-nullish.

---

### 3. Keys are coerced and can collide

Because this is an object, keys are coerced to property keys:

```ts
groupBy([{ k: 1 }, { k: "1" }], "k")
```

Both go into the same group.

Objects/functions also collide:

```ts
groupBy([{ k: {} }, { k: [] }], "k")
// both become "[object Object]" or similar string keys
```

`null` becomes `"null"`, `undefined` becomes `"undefined"`, etc.

Also, `keyof T` allows grouping by properties whose values are objects, arrays, functions, or optional/undefined values. That may be unintended.

---

### Recommended fix

Use a `Map`, which avoids prototype keys and preserves key types/identity:

```ts
function groupBy<T, K extends keyof T>(
  xs: readonly T[],
  key: K
): Map<T[K], T[]> {
  const groups = new Map<T[K], T[]>();

  for (const x of xs) {
    const k = x[key];
    const group = groups.get(k);

    if (group) {
      group.push(x);
    } else {
      groups.set(k, [x]);
    }
  }

  return groups;
}
```

If you absolutely must return a plain object, use a null-prototype object and explicit stringification:

```ts
function groupBy<T, K extends keyof T>(
  xs: readonly T[],
  key: K
): Record<string, T[]> {
  const groups = Object.create(null) as Record<string, T[]>;

  for (const x of xs) {
    const k = String(x[key]);
    (groups[k] ??= []).push(x);
  }

  return groups;
}
```

But the `Map` version is the safer default. The original is a compile-time error under strict settings and a runtime `TypeError` for prototype-property group names.

